        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: sternō, sternere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sternō, sternere — \"to spread, to lay out; to pave (a road)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fabrī viam novam saxīs sternunt. The workmen pave the new road with stones."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sternō, sternere, strāvī, strātum. Viam sternere, to pave a road; lectum sternere, to make a bed. Its participle strāta gives English street."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sternō is inherited from Proto-Italic stornō, from PIE sterh₃- 'to stretch out, to spread' (EtymDB). Its participle in via strāta 'paved road' was borrowed into Germanic and became English street (EtymDB)."
            }
          ]
        },
